<file path="api/knowledge/arts/color.md">
---
title: Color is a relationship
tag: arts
source: cognite-original
---

A patch of paint is not "blue" in isolation. Next to orange it can vibrate. Next to a darker blue it can look pale. Josef Albers built a whole teaching method on this: the same physical color appears to change when its neighbors change. Color is a relationship.

That is why palettes matter more than favorite pigments. Filmmakers, illustrators, and interior designers choose limited sets so the eye can learn a world. Complementary pairs create tension. Analogous neighbors create calm. A small warm accent in a cool field will steal attention every time, which is useful for a signal and disastrous for a noisy poster.

Light complicates the lesson. North daylight, tungsten bulbs, and phone screens all shift what you think you mixed. Painters learn to squint, to compare, to put a test stroke on the actual ground instead of trusting the tube's name.

You do not need to become a color theorist to use the idea. If a page feels chaotic, reduce the number of hues. If a scene feels dead, change the neighbor, not the hero. Seeing color as relation is another way of saying: nothing important sits alone. Context is part of the work.
</file>

<file path="api/knowledge/arts/constraints.md">
---
title: Constraints make style
tag: arts
source: cognite-original
---

A sonnet has fourteen lines. A blues verse has a familiar twelve-bar spine. A haiku counts beats. These are not handcuffs so much as games with rules. Constraints reduce the search space so a maker can finish, compare, and surprise inside a shared form.

Twentieth-century artists often invented their own constraints: a painter limited to three colors, a filmmaker shooting in one room, a novelist avoiding a letter. The Oulipo writers treated restriction as an engine. The point is not suffering. The point is that infinite choice is a poor collaborator.

Beginners sometimes wait to "feel free" before they start. Practitioners pick a box. Write 300 words. Use only photographs from one walk. Compose for a cheap keyboard. The box creates style because style is a pattern of decisions, and decisions need edges.

There is a warning attached. A constraint that you never chose — a feed's length, a template's layout, a client's panic — can also produce a style, just not yours. The artistic move is to notice which limits are instruments and which are someone else's default. Then steal the useful ones on purpose.
</file>

<file path="api/knowledge/arts/negative-space.md">
---
title: Negative space is a kind of drawing
tag: arts
source: cognite-original
---

Beginners often draw the object: the cup, the hand, the tree. Artists also draw the gaps — the triangle of wall between an arm and a torso, the light around a bowl. That habit is called attending to negative space. It is a trick for seeing relations instead of labels.

Once you look at the gap, proportions get easier because you stop fighting the word "hand," which your mind already thinks it knows. The same shift appears in music as rest, in architecture as courtyard, in theater as pause. What is not sounded or built can organize what is.

This is craft, not mysticism. A page of text with no margins is harder to read. A song with no breath is harder to feel. Designers talk about white space for the same reason sculptors talk about the air around a figure: attention needs a place to stand.

You can practice in two minutes. Look at a plant against a window and sketch only the sky-shaped pieces. Or listen to a piece of music and count the silences. The skill transfers. In writing, it is the cut sentence. In conversation, it is not rushing to fill a pause. Art trains perception, and perception is the raw material of every other kind of thinking.
</file>

<file path="api/knowledge/history/libraries.md">
---
title: Why libraries are radical
tag: history
source: cognite-original
---

A public library looks quiet. Historically, it is a radical idea: that ordinary people should be able to read without buying every book, proving membership in a guild, or asking a patron's permission. Access to texts has almost always been political because texts carry law, religion, science, and satire.

Medieval European libraries were often attached to monasteries or universities, with chained books and carefully controlled copying. Islamic cities such as Baghdad and Cordoba ran famous houses of learning that translated and extended Greek, Persian, and Indian works. In East Asia, imperial collections and exam culture made literacy a route to office. None of these systems was "the public" in the modern sense.

The nineteenth-century public library movement, especially in Britain and the United States, argued that a democracy needs readers. Industrial cities funded buildings, catalogs, and hours of free browsing. Andrew Carnegie later paid for thousands of library buildings, with local communities expected to stock and staff them. The gift was architecture plus a bet on civic maintenance.

Libraries still negotiate inclusion: which languages, which hours, whose history is on the shelf. Digital lending adds copyright fights to the older ones. The through-line is simple. A society that treats reading as a private luxury will get a different public than a society that treats it as infrastructure.
</file>

<file path="api/knowledge/history/printing-press.md">
---
title: The printing press as a network effect
tag: history
source: cognite-original
---

Johannes Gutenberg's press, developed in Mainz in the mid-1400s, combined movable metal type, oil-based ink, and a screw press. Printing itself was not new: China and Korea had earlier woodblock and metal-type traditions. What changed in Europe was speed of spread plus a market of vernacular readers, universities, and churches that wanted identical copies.

A book copied by hand is a performance. A printed book is a small factory run. Once type is set, the cost of the next copy drops. That makes pamphlets, indulgences, scientific tables, and news sheets economically possible. It also makes censorship a different problem: you can burn a manuscript; you cannot easily burn a whole edition that has already ridden out of town.

Historians still argue about how much the press "caused" the Reformation, the Scientific Revolution, or nationalism. A cautious claim is enough: cheaper identical texts made it easier to compare claims, quote chapter and verse, and notice contradictions. Maps, music, and mathematical notation benefited from the same trick.

The modern echo is not "screens are the new press" as a slogan. It is that communication technology changes who can publish, how fast error travels, and what counts as a shared reference. Movable type did not make people wise. It changed the cost of making an argument public.
</file>

<file path="api/knowledge/history/silk-roads.md">
---
title: Silk Roads were many roads
tag: history
source: cognite-original
---

"The Silk Road" is a later nickname, popularized in the nineteenth century, for a web of overland and maritime routes that linked East Asia, Central Asia, the Indian Ocean, the Middle East, and the Mediterranean. Silk moved, but so did glass, spices, horses, paper, religions, diseases, and stories.

Caravan cities such as Samarkand and Dunhuang grew as hubs, not as endpoints. Travelers rarely went the whole way. Goods and ideas hopped from merchant to merchant. That hopping is why a motif, a dye, or a parable can show up thousands of kilometers from its origin without a single famous explorer to credit.

The routes were never only peaceful commerce. Empires taxed them, bandits raided them, and climate and politics opened or closed passes. The Mongol period in the thirteenth and fourteenth centuries is often described as making long-distance travel safer for a time; it also moved armies and, likely, pathogens. The Black Death's geography is still studied with care, but connectedness cut both ways.

Remembering the Silk Roads as a network rather than a highway is the historical skill. Culture does not usually travel as a pure package. It is selected, mistranslated, and remixed at every stop. That is as true of recipes as of religions.
</file>

<file path="api/knowledge/philosophy/attention.md">
---
title: Attention as a moral problem
tag: philosophy
source: cognite-original
---

Simone Weil wrote that attention is the rarest and purest form of generosity. Iris Murdoch argued that moral life is less about heroic choices than about what we steadily look at. Both are pushing against a picture of ethics as a calculator you take out only in emergencies.

If that is right, then the design of your day is ethical design. What you repeatedly notice becomes easier to care about. What you never look at becomes abstract, including other people's inner lives. A feed that trains you to skim faces as content is not only a productivity issue.

This does not require a hair shirt. Attention is finite, so some filtering is sanity. The philosophical question is who sets the filter. If an interface always chooses the next object for you, you are practicing a kind of passivity. If you choose a book, a problem, or a conversation and return to it after wandering, you are practicing a kind of freedom — not metaphysical freedom, just authorship of the next few minutes.

Cognite's product bet sits here, not in neuroscience theater. A short reading is a small claim: you can pick an object of attention on purpose. Whether that makes you a better person is a longer argument. Whether it is possible is something you can test this afternoon.
</file>

<file path="api/knowledge/philosophy/examined-life.md">
---
title: The examined life is a method
tag: philosophy
source: cognite-original
---

Socrates, as Plato presents him, walks around Athens asking people what they mean by courage, justice, or piety. The point is not to win a quiz. It is to show that confident definitions often collapse when you test them with counterexamples. "The unexamined life is not worth living" is easy to quote and hard to practice, because examination threatens status.

Socratic method, stripped of marble statues, is a loop: state a claim, ask what it implies, look for a case that does not fit, revise. It is closer to debugging than to preaching. You can use it on a political slogan, a career plan, or the sentence "I do not have time to read."

Two mistakes follow the slogan. One is endless doubt that never chooses an action. The other is treating examination as a personality — being the person who always asks a clever question at dinner. The useful middle is local: pick one belief that actually guides your week and pressure-test it.

Philosophy here is not a rival to science. It is care with concepts. If you cannot say what you mean by "focus," "waste," or "a good day," you will still live by some definition. It will just be an unexamined one, usually borrowed from whatever interface is nearest.
</file>

<file path="api/knowledge/philosophy/thought-experiments.md">
---
title: Thought experiments are not evidence
tag: philosophy
source: cognite-original
---

A thought experiment is a carefully designed imaginary case: a runaway trolley, a brain in a vat, a violinist plugged into your circulatory system overnight. Philosophers use them to isolate a principle. If you say "always minimize deaths," the trolley case asks whether you would still say that when minimizing deaths means diverting a train yourself.

The method is powerful and abused. It is powerful because real life arrives messy, with extra facts that let you dodge the principle. It is abused when the story is so science-fictional that your intuition is just a reaction to weirdness. "I would not push the large person" might be a moral judgment, a disgust response, or a refusal to accept the premise.

Good use of thought experiments treats them as questions, not as data. They reveal what you are already committed to. They do not replace history, psychology, or the details of an actual hospital. When a public debate is conducted only in hypotheticals, ask what real situation is being avoided.

You can steal the tool without the jargon. Before you adopt a rule ("I never skip," "I always optimize"), invent a case where the rule looks cruel or silly. If you still want the rule, keep it. If not, you have learned something cheaply, without waiting for the world to stage the example.
</file>

<file path="api/knowledge/science/feedback-loops.md">
---
title: Feedback loops in ordinary life
tag: science
source: cognite-original
---

A feedback loop happens when a system's output becomes part of its later input. A thermostat is the classroom example: the heater warms the room, a sensor reads the temperature, and the heater shuts off. That is negative feedback, which stabilizes. A microphone too close to a speaker is positive feedback: sound in, louder sound out, until the room rings.

Biology is dense with both. Insulin helps bring high blood sugar down. Hunger and satiety hormones push eating up or down. Predator and prey populations can oscillate. Climate systems include loops too: melting ice can expose darker water that absorbs more heat, which melts more ice.

The scientific habit is to ask which loop you are in before you "fix" a number. Pushing on a stabilizing loop can look like nothing is happening, until it suddenly is. Pushing on a reinforcing loop can look like rapid success, until it overshoots.

You do not need laboratory equipment to use the idea. Sleep, caffeine, scrolling, and mood can form loops. A late night makes the next day harder, which makes another late night tempting. Naming the loop does not automatically break it, but it gives you a place to intervene: earlier bedtime, a walk, a different first action in the morning. Science, here, is a way of seeing circular causes instead of a single villain.
</file>

<file path="api/knowledge/science/photosynthesis.md">
---
title: Photosynthesis in one tight story
tag: science
source: cognite-original
---

Photosynthesis is how most of the living world captures sunlight and stores it as chemical energy. In plants, algae, and many bacteria, pigments such as chlorophyll absorb photons. That energy is used to split water, release oxygen, and build energy carriers. Those carriers then help stitch carbon dioxide into sugars.

The story has two linked acts. The light-dependent reactions happen in membranes and produce ATP, NADPH, and oxygen. The Calvin cycle, which does not need photons at every step but needs the products of the first act, fixes carbon. Glucose and other carbohydrates are later used for growth, storage, or respiration.

This is why plants matter far beyond gardens. They are an interface between the sun and the food web. The oxygen in the air you are breathing is a waste product of water-splitting that accumulated over geological time. Fossil fuels are, roughly, ancient photosynthesis packed into a dense, slow-to-renew form.

A common confusion is to treat plants as "making energy from nothing." They do not. They convert one form of energy into another, with losses, like every other physical process. The elegance is the conversion, not a violation of thermodynamics. When you eat a fruit, you are borrowing sunlight that was captured on a leaf.
</file>

<file path="api/knowledge/science/working-memory.md">
---
title: Working memory has a small stage
tag: science
source: cognite-original
---

Working memory is the small amount of information you can hold in mind and manipulate at once: a phone number you are about to dial, the beginning of a sentence you are still writing, the rule you are using to sort objects. It is not the same as long-term memory, which can store languages, faces, and routes for years.

Laboratory tasks suggest the stage is limited. Classic estimates talked about "seven plus or minus two" chunks. Later work often finds fewer, especially if the items are novel and you cannot group them. Chunking is the trick: "C-I-A" is three letters or one agency. Expertise is, in part, better chunking.

Attention and working memory are close neighbors. Distraction does not always erase knowledge; it can simply knock the current items off the stage. That is one reason a notification during a proof, a recipe, or a delicate conversation feels costly. You may still "know" the material, but you have to reload it.

None of this requires claiming that an app can see your networks fire. The practical implication is architectural. If a task needs several pieces held together — an argument, a budget, a design constraint — protect a few quiet minutes. Externalize what you can onto paper. Build one chunk at a time. The brain's stage is small. Good work respects the furniture.
</file>

<file path="api/knowledge/technology/apis.md">
---
title: Why APIs feel like doors
tag: technology
source: cognite-original
---

An API, or application programming interface, is a contract. One program promises that if you ask for something in a particular shape, it will answer in a particular shape. You do not need to know how the other program stores data, which language it uses, or which server room it lives in. You only need the contract.

That is why APIs feel like doors. A well-designed door has a handle you can find in the dark. A well-designed API has endpoints, methods, and error messages that stay stable. When the door is moved without warning, every caller trips. That is the social meaning of "breaking change."

REST-style HTTP APIs became popular because the web already knew how to GET, POST, PUT, and DELETE resources. JSON became a common body format because it is readable by both humans and machines. None of this is magic. It is a shared vocabulary that lets a Chrome extension talk to a local server, or a weather app talk to a national agency.

The important design choice is not cleverness. It is what you refuse to expose. An API that accepts a user's interests and a reading length does not need the URL of the page they were scrolling. Smaller contracts leak less, fail less, and stay easier to reason about. Technology is often improved by sending less.
</file>

<file path="api/knowledge/technology/caching.md">
---
title: Caching is a bet about the future
tag: technology
source: cognite-original
---

A cache stores an answer you think you will need again. Your browser caches images so a second visit is faster. A CPU caches recently used memory so it does not walk all the way to RAM. A news app caches headlines so it can open on a train.

Every cache is a bet: that the future will look enough like the past. When the bet is right, you save time and energy. When it is wrong, you show stale data, serve an old price, or hide a security fix. Cache invalidation — deciding when a stored answer is no longer trustworthy — is famously hard because the world does not send a single, clean "this changed" signal.

There are simple policies. Time-to-live expires an item after a number of minutes. Least-recently-used discards whatever nobody asked for. Write-through updates the cache whenever the source of truth changes. None of these policies is universally best. A weather forecast can be a few minutes late. A bank balance should not be.

For a small product, the useful lesson is restraint. Cache what is expensive and slow to change. Do not cache secrets in a place you cannot lock. And remember that a cache is not knowledge; it is a convenience copy. If you cannot explain how an item becomes invalid, you are not caching. You are hoping.
</file>

<file path="api/knowledge/technology/version-control.md">
---
title: Version control as a time machine
tag: technology
source: cognite-original
---

Version control is easy to treat as a backup button. Its deeper idea is that software is a history of decisions. A commit is a snapshot plus a message about why the snapshot exists. A branch is a parallel history. A merge is a negotiation between two histories that both claim to be true.

Git, the most common tool for this, stores snapshots of a project as a directed graph of commits. Each commit points to its parent (or parents). That graph lets you reconstruct any past state without keeping an endless stack of duplicate folders. It also makes collaboration possible: two people can change the same project, then reconcile those changes instead of overwriting one another.

Three practical habits matter more than advanced commands. First, commit in small, coherent pieces so the history is readable. Second, write messages that explain the reason for a change, not only the file list. Third, review diffs before you share them. A diff is a map of what actually moved, which is often more honest than memory.

Conflicts feel like failures, but they are the system doing its job. Git cannot know which of two edits to a paragraph is the intended one. A human has to decide. The same is true of product choices: tools can record options, but they cannot pick the goal. Version control does not invent intelligence. It externalizes memory so your attention can stay on the next decision.
</file>

